<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cần Vương</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Can Vuong movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cần Vương ('Aid the King') movement was a Vietnamese uprising against French rule launched in July 1885, when the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ton-that-thuyet">Tôn Thất Thuyết</a> fled Huế with the boy emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ham-nghi">Hàm Nghi</a> and issued an edict calling on scholars and people to resist. Led by local scholar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, it fought in central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>; Hàm Nghi was captured in 1888, and the last major leader, Phan Đình Phùng, died in 1895.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/can-vuong.jpg" alt="Cần Vương" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nguyen Dynasty in 19th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chieu_Can_Vuong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In its early years it fought the <b>Can Vuong</b> resistance and armed bands in the Tonkin uplands, notably the forces of De Tham in Yen The, who held out until 1913.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Yet the dynasty also produced resistance: the <b>Can Vuong</b> (‘Aid the King’) movement launched in the name of the boy emperor Ham Nghi in 1885, and the emperors Thanh Thai and Duy Tan, deposed and exiled by the French in 1907 and 1916, became patriotic symbols.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>France deposed emperors who resisted, including Ham Nghi, who led the <b>Can Vuong movement</b> from 1885, and later Thanh Thai and Duy Tan.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-annam">French protectorate of Annam</a></li></ul></section>
